{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey Eviction Law good cause and notice requirements","requirement":"A landlord may evict a residential tenant only for good cause listed in the Eviction Law, such as a criminal conviction or civil-court liability for assault, threats, theft or drug activity on the premises, and must serve a Notice to Quit before suing.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants generally; landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings have their own causes and notice requirements under N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2, including three-month notice for at-will or year-to-year tenancies and one-month notice for month-to-month tenancies.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings have separate eviction causes and notice rules"},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings are removed under a separate cause scheme (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2).","penalty":"A landlord who, after an owner-occupancy eviction, does not occupy the unit for six months is liable for three times the damages plus attorney fees; a landlord who permanently removes the unit from residential use and reuses it residentially within five years faces treble damages, fees and a civil penalty up to $10,000 for each violation.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may file for eviction, if the tenant has been convicted of or pleaded guilty to, or if a juvenile has been found to be delinquent by the Court due to an offense involving theft of property from the landlord or from tenants residing in the same building or complex.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey anti-reprisal protection for tenants","requirement":"A landlord may not evict a tenant, substantially alter a lease or its terms, or refuse to renew a lease in reprisal for the tenant enforcing lease or legal rights, complaining to a government authority about health or safety violations, or joining a tenant organization.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Excludes owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"The tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey tenant protections in foreclosure","requirement":"A buyer who acquires a foreclosed property with residential rental units must give the tenant a 90-day notice to quit, and must notify tenants in English and Spanish within 10 business days after the sale that ownership changed and they need not move.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Any person acquiring a foreclosed property containing one or more residential rental units; month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units are not exempt from the 90-day notice.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Foreclosed properties with one or more residential rental units"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69","quoted_span":"Before a tenant can be evicted due to foreclosure, the landlord must provide the tenant with a 90 -day notice to qui t when the foreclosed property has been purchased by a buyer who wants to personally occupy it as his or her primary residence.","interaction":"Federal foreclosure law does not preempt State or local law giving tenants longer time periods or other additional protections.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act","requirement":"Tenants at least 62, permanently disabled, or certain disabled veterans who lived in a building being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership for at least one year before the conversion recording may be protected from eviction for 40 years.","key_value":"40 years","coverage_conditions":"Buildings being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership; tenants must be at least 62 before the conversion recording date, permanently disabled, or honorably discharged veterans 60% or more disabled from service, and residents for at least one year.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Buildings being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":"Tenants whose family income exceeds three times the county average per-person income or $50,000, whichever is greater.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever  is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992","requirement":"Qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible as senior citizens or disabled persons under the 1981 Act receive protected tenancy, limiting eviction.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership; currently only Hudson County is a qualified county.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Qualified counties; currently only Hudson County"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house eviction requirements","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good-cause and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, unless the Bureau orders otherwise, and must give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting an eviction action.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Rooming and boarding houses: buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms, unless an exception in the Rooming and Boarding House Act applies.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Rooming and boarding houses"},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)). There is a further requirement that the owner give at least three (3) working days’ notice to the County Welfare Board before starting the eviction action for any resident  (N.J.A.C. 5:26-3.4(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
